  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers tonight, cooler and dry on Friday in the \nwake of the cold front.\n\nA low amplitude mid level trough will advance east across Quebec \ntonight, with an associated surface cold front moving southeast \nacross the eastern Great Lakes this evening. Large scale forcing is \nweak, but a focused ribbon of low level convergence along the front \nand a narrow axis of sufficient moisture will support a band of \nshowers moving from northwest to southeast across the area through \nthe first half of tonight. The fast moving nature of the system will \nkeep rainfall amounts less than 0.25\" in most areas, and less than \n0.10\" across the Genesee Valley and Finger Lakes as the \nsouthwesterly boundary layer flow downslopes off the higher terrain \nof the western Southern Tier. Rain will quickly end from northwest \nto southeast after midnight following the cold frontal passage.\n\nCool northwest flow aloft and associated upslope flow and shallow \nlake induced instability will support plenty of cloud cover through \nat least midday Friday, with only partial/gradual clearing in the \nafternoon. Temperatures will be much cooler than the past few days \nbut very close to average for the end of October.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another extended period of dry weather and above \naverage temperatures this weekend through early next week.\n\nA mid/upper level ridge will quickly build back into the central and \neastern CONUS this weekend and persist through early next week as a \ndeep trough moves slowly east from the Rockies. Surface high \npressure will settle over the eastern Great Lakes Saturday, then \ngradually drift off the southern New England coast Sunday through \nMonday. This feature will maintain dry weather across the eastern \nGreat Lakes with temperatures trending above average.\n\nA more active pattern may develop by the middle to end of next week \nas a deep trough moves slowly east across the CONUS, possibly \nevolving into a closed low as it reaches the Ohio Valley and Great \nLakes. There is considerable spread in ensemble members and \ndeterministic model runs with the timing of this system, but once it \narrives it will likely bring a wetter pattern that lasts for at \nleast a few days later next week.",
